Release	UNKNOWN STEP	2026-10-01T19:03:22.8196606Z [{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/pulls/111","id":4681145759,"node_id":"PR_kwDOQYTTi88AAAABFwSdnw","html_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/111","diff_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/111.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/111.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/link-foundation/browser-commander/issues/111","number":111,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Seed browser profiles and close native parity gaps across JavaScript, Python and Rust","user":{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"This PR delivers #110 in a single change: it fixes #107 and #108, and makes #109 ready for its first release. #109 needs one PyPI account action that no repository credential can perform; see below.\n\n## Browser profile controls (#107)\n\nFresh real-browser launches now seed `Preferences` and `Local State` before Chrome-family browsers start, so the default-browser prompt is gone without adding a switch. The command line stays the two original switches.\n\n- Options and CLI flags cover arbitrary preferences and Local State (`--pref`, `--local-state`), `defaultBrowserCheck` (`--default-browser-check`) and `firstRun`, in JavaScript, Python and Rust.\n- Settings are reapplied after a migration or snapshot copy, including to the selected profile.\n- Existing argument behavior and opt-ins are unchanged.\n- [Browser profile settings](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/browser-profile-settings.md) lists every value written by default, with its opt-out, and gives the enterprise-policy story for each OS. Policies are inherited from the OS; Chromium has no safe profile-local managed-policy store to write.\n\n**Reproduction and verification:**\n- A fresh headful Chrome 153 profile still showed the default-browser infobar with only the older preference set.\n- Seeding both Local State decline counters as well removes it.\n- The [native-window capture](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/experiments/issue-110/capture-infobar.py) captures the whole browser window, not the page. It measures 5,073 infobar pixels before the fix and zero after, and CI runs it under Xvfb.\n- Edge was captured the same way. Brave is not installed on the test host.\n\n| Before | After |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![Chrome before](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/screenshots/issue-110/chrome-before.png?raw=true) | ![Chrome after](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/screenshots/issue-110/chrome-after.png?raw=true) |\n\n## Native and typed parity (#108)\n\nEach numbered requirement of #108 is listed below. The [requirement inventory](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/issue-110-analysis.md) maps each one to its code and tests.\n\n1. **Rust Playwright through the official driver.**\n   - command-stream's binary-safe stdin/stdout API starts `playwright-core/cli.js run-driver`.\n   - The typed client in `rust/src/playwright/protocol/` is generated from Playwright's protocol spec (v1.62.1).\n   - `PlaywrightDriverPage` implements `EngineAdapter`, and `page.objects()` exposes the typed `Browser`, `BrowserContext`, `Page` and `Frame` channels.\n   - The Node bridge is kept only as a fallback, for when no matching `playwright-core` is installed.\n   - Checks: `playwright-protocol-coverage.test.js` fails on any missing interface, command or event, or a stale spec; `rust/tests/playwright_driver.rs` runs against real Chrome.\n2. **Typed Rust and Python Puppeteer generated from `.d.ts`.**\n   - `scripts/puppeteer-api.mjs` reads puppeteer-core's `lib/types.d.ts` with the TypeScript compiler API. It finds 46 classes and interfaces.\n   - `generate-puppeteer-bindings.mjs` turns that into typed Rust structs and Python classes over the bridge, one async method per member.\n   - Checks: `puppeteer-bindings-coverage.test.js` fails when a declared method has no typed entry point. Fake-server unit tests and real-Chrome tests run in both languages.\n3. **Native trace recording in Python and Rust.** Both produce the same `.bc-trace` bundle, offline viewer and Links Notation export as JavaScript.\n   - **What is recorded:** checkpoints; ordered DOM mutation batches that survive navigation; live form state; stable identities; one ordered timeline; redaction before anything is written; size-limited partial bundles. Retain-on-failure is `traced()` in Python and `record_scenario` in Rust.\n   - **Shared code:** the in-page capture functions and the viewer are not rewritten. They are generated from `js/src/traces/` into `assets.json`, and a test fails when that copy is stale.\n   - **Rust plumbing:** chromiumoxide feeds the recorder through the new `EngineAdapter::trace_events` stream (navigation, console, exceptions, failed requests, dialogs) and init scripts.\n   - **Remaining Rust gaps (marked in the [Portable Traces table](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/feature-parity.md#portable-traces)):** mutation batches are drained from the main frame only, and chromiumoxide reports no downloads.\n   - **Checks:** `generate-trace-conformance.mjs` records one scenario with the JavaScript recorder. `test_conformance.py` and `rust/tests/trace_conformance.rs` replay it and must produce byte-identical bundle, viewer and `.lino` files, and redact a 21-URL corpus the same way. Ported recorder, bundle, viewer, Links, redaction and retention tests run in both languages. Real-Chrome recordings run in the Browser Parity workflow: Python covers typing, a password, a dialog, a navigation and DOM changes; Rust covers a navigation, typing, a password, DOM changes, console output and a page error.\n4. **Rust Selenium.**\n   - ChromeDriver and geckodriver are launched through command-stream.\n   - The complete typed Fantoccini client is exposed, with optional BiDi, managed downloads, copied profiles and cleanup.\n   - Real Chrome and Firefox tests pass. Firefox `.part` files stay pending until finalized.\n   - A reproducing test found a driver process surviving Tokio shutdown; the final cleanup is now synchronous. See [Native WebDriver](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/native-webdriver.md).\n5. **Portable state, snapshot attach, extension relay and `measureParity`** are now native in every engine and language.\n   - **Portable state:** Playwright-compatible cookie/localStorage state is imported and exported everywhere, with real-browser transfer tests across engines.\n   - **Snapshots:** `snapshot_user_data_dir` and `launch_snapshot` keep committed WAL data, report what they excluded, and delete their copy on close.\n     - An exclusive-lock regression is fixed. Rust's three-engine test went from 237 seconds to 9.\n   - **Parity measurement:** Rust has `measure_parity` and `measure_session_parity`, with a command-stream reference browser.\n     - The probe caught Puppeteer's viewport emulation, caused by stripping `defaultViewport: null`. All three engines now report zero differences.\n   - **Extension relay:** Python and Rust host it without Node.\n     - A reproducing test caught an immediate-close hang in Rust, which is fixed.\n6. **Python process execution.**\n   - The command-stream equivalent is documented, and its streaming and cancellation are tested.\n   - Cancelling a command now terminates and reaps its child.\n7. **Generated matrix tiers.**\n   - Every claim is labelled Native typed, Typed via bridge or Untyped via CLI.\n   - CI rejects missing labels, stale limitations and CLI-only claims without a technical reason.\n   - One documented gap is left: the CLI script and stdio bridge take dynamic JSON by design.\n\nFound and fixed along the way:\n- **XSS in the offline trace viewer.** CodeQL flagged it (`js/xss-through-dom`). The viewer inserted a recorded page's URL, title, control values and event fields into its markup unescaped. Its policy allows inline handlers, so a page titled `<img src=x onerror=…>` ran script in the viewer of whoever opened the trace. The viewer now escapes that text, and a test runs the viewer script against a stand-in document to check it. The fix applies to all three languages, because they share the viewer.\n  - CodeQL's remaining alert there (#55) is the mutation replay, which parses recorded HTML with `DOMParser` by design. That document is inert, and its output only reaches the stage frame, which is sandboxed without `allow-scripts`. [`viewer-replay-inert.mjs`](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/experiments/issue-110/viewer-replay-inert.mjs) opens a hostile recording in Chrome. Before the fix, the URL and title ran; now nothing runs, including the replayed mutation. The alert is dismissed as a false positive.\n- **Python `Locator.first` crash.** Python's `Locator.first` is a property, but several helpers called it like a method. On a real browser this raised `TypeError`, and `is_enabled` swallowed it, so it reported enabled elements as disabled. The real-browser trace test exposed this, and `test_playwright_locator_api.py` reproduces it.\n- **Windows line endings in Python traces.** Python trace files were opened in Windows text mode, which turned `\\n` into `\\r\\n`, so the Windows conformance run failed. They are now written in binary mode, and `test_opens_every_member_in_binary_mode` covers it.\n\n## Python publication (#109)\n\nThe repository side is done:\n- The README badge and install instructions are truthful. They give a source install until the first release.\n- The release workflow reports a missing publisher (`invalid-publisher`) as a setup step with a link.\n\nOne step is outside what this PR can do. `https://pypi.org/pypi/browser-commander/json` still returns 404 because no trusted publisher is registered. A PyPI account owner has to register a pending publisher with:\n\n| Field | Value |\n| --- | --- |\n| Project | `browser-commander` |\n| Owner | `link-foundation` |\n| Repository | `browser-commander` |\n| Workflow | `python.yml` |\n| Environment | empty |\n\nAfter that, run `gh workflow run python.yml`. That form needs a signed-in PyPI account, and GitHub credentials do not grant one. [Setup details](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/pull/111#issuecomment-5896799630).\n\n## Validation\n\nAll 10 workflows pass on `daa712a`, on Linux, macOS and Windows, and the CodeQL check reports no new alerts.\n\n- **JavaScript:** 1,486 tests pass on Linux and macOS. On Windows, 1,479 pass and 7 skip with a stated platform reason, such as symlinks and directory modes. Lint, formatting, the duplication baseline and npm audit pass.\n- **Python:** 1,075 tests pass on Linux (8 opt-in browser tests skipped), 1,074 on macOS and 1,070 on Windows, where the remaining tests are platform skips. Ruff, formatting, mypy and the coverage checks pass.\n- **Rust:** 670 tests pass on Linux, 669 on macOS and 658 on Windows, where platform-specific tests are not built. On each OS, 15 real-browser tests are ignored; they run in Browser Parity. Clippy and rustdoc pass with warnings denied, and `rustfmt` passes. The OpenSSL-free build and coverage jobs also pass.\n- **Browser Parity:** real Chrome and Firefox tests pass, including `trace_record_real_browser` and the snapshot, parity, WebDriver, Playwright driver and Puppeteer bridge tests.\n- **Generated-file gates:** the feature matrix, trace assets and the trace conformance fixtures are regenerated and must match. The file-size, required-docs, shared-asset, workflow-policy, link, secretlint and `git diff --check` gates pass.\n- **Branch:** it contains the current `main`, and release fragments are included for every package.\n\nThe [CI investigation](https://github.com/link-foundation/browser-commander/blob/issue-110-1b63faff9765/docs/issue-110-ci-investigation.md) records the failed runs, their exact errors and the fixes.\n\nFixes #110\nFixes #107\nFixes #108\nFixes #109\n","created_at":"2026-09-29T18:42:20Z","updated_at":"2026-10-01T19:01:59Z","closed_at":"2026-10-01T19:00:31Z","merged_at":"2026-10-01T19:00:31Z","merge_commit_sha":"6764d1fad9822fe55ec132e38bec7681f4e16541","assignees":[{"login":"konard","id":1431904,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzE5MDQ=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1431904?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard","html_url":"https://github.com/konard","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/konard/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.gith